1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,600
Zeus had overthrown a father
who swallowed his own children.

2
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:09,720
Then he took a wife — and heard the
same prophecy his father had heard.

3
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:14,940
Her name was Metis, and she was
wiser than any god or mortal.

4
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:20,360
Earth and Sky, the same two who had warned
Cronus, told Zeus what she would bear.

5
00:00:20,640 --> 00:00:23,860
First a daughter, his equal
in strength and judgment.

6
00:00:24,480 --> 00:00:28,720
Then a son, who would
be king of gods and men.

7
00:00:29,540 --> 00:00:31,260
Zeus did not wait for the son.

8
00:00:32,180 --> 00:00:35,680
He tricked Metis with soft
words, and swallowed her whole.

9
00:00:36,260 --> 00:00:39,360
His father had swallowed the
children, and one got away.

10
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:41,460
Zeus swallowed the mother.

11
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:44,580
But the daughter was already inside her.

12
00:00:44,580 --> 00:00:49,020
In time, Zeus gave birth to her
himself — out of his own head.

13
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:52,980
Later poets say it took
an axe to split it open.

14
00:00:53,180 --> 00:00:57,200
Out leapt a grown woman in
golden armour, shaking a spear.

15
00:00:58,040 --> 00:01:00,220
Olympus shook, the sea heaved,

16
00:01:00,220 --> 00:01:04,580
and the Sun stopped his horses in the
sky until she took the armour off.

17
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:06,220
This was Athena.

18
00:01:06,580 --> 00:01:08,940
Her mother's name meant cunning wisdom,

19
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:12,360
and Athena became its
goddess, in craft and in war.

20
00:01:12,720 --> 00:01:14,220
And Metis never left.

21
00:01:15,160 --> 00:01:19,580
Hesiod says Zeus kept her inside
him, to counsel him on good and evil.

22
00:01:19,980 --> 00:01:25,340
When the gods chose their cities, Athena
and her uncle Poseidon both wanted Athens.

23
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:30,940
He struck the rock of the Acropolis with
his trident, and salt water welled up.

24
00:01:31,140 --> 00:01:32,940
She planted an olive tree.

25
00:01:33,820 --> 00:01:36,160
The gods gave her the city, and Poseidon,

26
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:40,100
the brother who never accepted his
share, flooded the plain in anger.

27
00:01:40,680 --> 00:01:44,909
The daughter of the swallowed wife
became her father's favourite — in one

28
00:01:44,909 --> 00:01:49,480
Athenian tragedy, the only god trusted
with the keys to his thunderbolt.

29
00:01:50,520 --> 00:01:53,960
Hera could not stop a child
born from her husband's head.

30
00:01:54,700 --> 00:01:57,400
A child carried by another
woman was different.

31
00:01:58,540 --> 00:02:02,620
Leto was pregnant with Zeus's twins,
and Hera hunted her across the earth.

32
00:02:03,640 --> 00:02:08,020
Land after land turned her away;
none dared let her give birth.

33
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:11,628
At last she reached
Delos — a bare rock that,

34
00:02:11,628 --> 00:02:14,180
in some tellings,
floated loose on the sea.

35
00:02:14,180 --> 00:02:19,140
Even Delos was afraid the god would
despise it and kick it into the waves.

36
00:02:20,360 --> 00:02:25,280
So Leto swore an oath: his temple
would make Delos rich forever.

37
00:02:26,360 --> 00:02:29,440
Her labour lasted nine
days and nine nights.

38
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:31,992
Every great goddess came — except Hera,

39
00:02:31,992 --> 00:02:35,860
who kept the goddess of
childbirth beside her on Olympus.

40
00:02:36,700 --> 00:02:39,040
The others sent a messenger
to fetch her in secret,

41
00:02:39,040 --> 00:02:43,020
with a bribe: a golden
necklace, nine cubits long.

42
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:48,600
The moment she set foot on Delos, Leto
threw her arms around a palm tree,

43
00:02:48,720 --> 00:02:53,000
the earth laughed beneath her, and
the child sprang out into the light.

44
00:02:53,340 --> 00:02:54,620
It was Apollo.

45
00:02:54,860 --> 00:02:59,620
The whole island blossomed with gold,
and the newborn god spoke at once.

46
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:02,560
The lyre and the bow
would be his, he said,

47
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:05,520
and he would tell
mankind the will of Zeus.

48
00:03:06,200 --> 00:03:09,340
In a later telling, his
twin sister Artemis was

49
00:03:09,340 --> 00:03:12,880
born first — and helped
her mother deliver him.

50
00:03:13,100 --> 00:03:16,260
She grew up a hunter,
unmarried, at home in the wild.

51
00:03:16,920 --> 00:03:20,000
Her mother had been turned
away by every land on earth.

52
00:03:20,820 --> 00:03:23,340
Now every woman in labour called on her.

53
00:03:24,420 --> 00:03:29,360
Apollo founded his oracle at Delphi, where
he killed the serpent that lived there.

54
00:03:29,960 --> 00:03:32,860
For a thousand years,
cities asked him what was

55
00:03:32,860 --> 00:03:36,040
coming before they went to
war or founded colonies.

56
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:38,240
And at Delphi, Hesiod says,

57
00:03:38,240 --> 00:03:42,240
Zeus had set up the stone his
father swallowed in his place.

58
00:03:42,440 --> 00:03:46,420
The god of prophecy kept the stone
where this whole story began.

59
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:51,920
Centuries later, Athens ruled that no
one could be born on Delos, or die there.

60
00:03:52,140 --> 00:03:54,560
The one land that let
Leto give birth became

61
00:03:54,560 --> 00:03:57,360
the one place in Greece
where nobody could.

62
00:03:57,740 --> 00:04:00,540
None of the three was supposed to be born.

63
00:04:00,920 --> 00:04:05,460
One came out of her father's head and ended
up holding the keys to his thunderbolt.

64
00:04:05,640 --> 00:04:08,060
Two came off a drifting
rock and became the

65
00:04:08,060 --> 00:04:10,680
goddess of birth and
the voice of prophecy.

66
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:15,040
The only child who never came was
the son who would have been king.

67
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:17,880
His mother is still inside Zeus.

68
00:04:18,680 --> 00:04:21,660
Cronus swallowed his children
to escape a prophecy.

69
00:04:21,980 --> 00:04:25,400
His son swallowed a wife
to escape the same one.

70
00:04:26,160 --> 00:04:29,716
In the next part, the god his
mother threw off Olympus marries

71
00:04:29,716 --> 00:04:33,220
the most beautiful goddess
— and catches her in a net.

72
00:04:34,060 --> 00:04:36,723
Athena, Apollo or Artemis
— which one would the

73
00:04:36,723 --> 00:04:39,620
world miss most if they
had never been born?

74
00:04:40,440 --> 00:04:41,840
Tell us in the comments.

75
00:04:42,280 --> 00:04:46,980
If you like stories like this one, leave
a like and subscribe to Animated Tales.

76
00:04:47,660 --> 00:04:48,836
Until next time.
